// What people are saying: a snapshot of Slack threads and Jira tickets that bear on CVR, from the last 48 hours only (Mon Nov 2, about 8 AM ET to Wed Nov 4, about 8 AM ET). Re-read Wed Nov 4 ~8 AM ET from 4:40 PM Nov 3: #ask-deap-bot-squad, #promotions and #team-cs-issues (one internal test-order cancellation ask, not CVR-relevant) had nothing new; #released had one post (Redbubble search pagination expanded from 10 to 20 pages, an SEO change, not CVR-relevant yet); #peak_daily_performance_standup had the Redbubble search style-swap thread, added as a card. The Nov 1 sale-plan card aged out. Re-read Tue Nov 3 ~2:40 PM ET: #released, #promotions and #team-cs-issues empty since 8:45 AM; #peak_daily_performance_standup only a standing-agenda note; #ask-deap-bot-squad had the Nov 2 daily bot checks, a plan to bring Redbubble bot defenses up to TeePublic's level, and a Googlebot rate-limit ticket (DEAP-6991, SEO crawl, logged as a bot note); the marketing analytics Nov 2 read and paid search bidding changes (#all-marketing-analytics-ai, Nov 3 10:20 AM) replaced the Redbubble 48-hour countdown banner card (shipping, not live). Beam checkout placement went live 1:37 PM (roadmap latest update, not a card). Earlier: re-read Tue Nov 3 ~8:45 AM ET: nothing new since Mon Nov 2 ~4:40 PM in #released, #promotions, #ask-deap-bot-squad or #team-cs-issues; #peak_daily_performance_standup had only a note that the dashboard now splits A/B tests by device; the Customer working group takeaways (Mon Nov 2, 4:46 PM) replaced the Poland-based fulfiller card (still live, tracked on the roadmap and Recent impact). Re-read Nov 2 ~4:40 PM ET: three new items since 2:40 PM (a marketing note in #peak_daily_performance_standup that Redbubble sessions are rising on a low-converting Pinterest mix, a TeePublic EU fulfiller launch in #released, a refreshed Favorites page now on for admins in #released); #promotions and #team-cs-issues empty; #ask-deap-bot-squad no posts after 10:41 AM; the Oct 31 to Nov 1 email add-to-cart card aged out of the three-card limit. Earlier read Nov 2 ~2:40 PM ET: the Oct 31 customer service card (orders charged without a confirmation, posted 11:53 AM Oct 31) aged out with no follow-up; nothing new that bears on CVR since 8:45 AM (#released and #promotions empty; #peak_daily_performance_standup only new members; #ask-deap-bot-squad had the automated Nov 1 daily bot checks, TeePublic low and Redbubble moderate but not meaningful for CVR; #team-cs-issues routine label and partner-order questions; a Redbubble email click-tracking record swap and the data pipeline incident fix do not change the numbers shown). Re-read Nov 2 ~8:45 AM ET: nothing new that bears on CVR (#ask-deap-bot-squad, #promotions, #peak_daily_performance_standup and #team-cs-issues empty since 7:30 AM; #released had one TeePublic artist program launch, not a CVR item). Earlier read Nov 2 ~7:45 AM ET: #ask-deap-bot-squad, #released and #promotions had no new posts since Oct 30 afternoon; #peak_daily_performance_standup's last post was Oct 30 7:13 PM ET (outside the window); no Granola meetings Oct 31 to Nov 2. Cards come from #promotions (November sale plan) and #proj-martech-consolidation.
const SLACK = [
  { icon: "🏷️", site: "TP", title: "TeePublic gross profit is running behind plan, driven by discounts", when: "Nov 4", url: "https://grid-articore.enterprise.slack.com/archives/C0C9X6MHV89/p1793811473878359", refs: [{"go": "tp|overall", "l": "TeePublic CVR"}], status: "Open", tone: "open", channel: "#peak_daily_performance_standup", fx: "No: gross profit margin, not CVR or sales. Lower discount levels could trade some orders for more profit per order.", body: "Gross profit margin on TeePublic was up on last year on Nov 3 but still behind the plan for a sale day, with the shortfall coming from the discount rate and the base margin on undiscounted products. Shipping margin is on plan. Marketing is looking at updating the stackable new-account offer to cut discount use, which needs product help and an analysis first, and another leader suggested solving the short term right away by reducing the offer.", tldr: "TeePublic margin is behind plan because of discounts, and a smaller stackable sign-up offer is the first lever on the table.", pts: ["Margin was better than last year but missed the plan for the day.", "The gap comes from the discount rate and base product margin, not shipping.", "The stackable new-account offer may be cut or limited; analysis comes first."], qs: ["Does a smaller offer cost enough orders to cancel the extra margin?"], why: "If TeePublic orders per visitor dip after a coupon change, check the new-account offer before looking for a site issue." },
  { icon: "🗳️", site: "Both", title: "Meta ad prices are falling after Election Day, but less than hoped", when: "Nov 4", url: "https://grid-articore.enterprise.slack.com/archives/C0C9X6MHV89/p1793806383964839", refs: [{"go": "tp|channel/Paid Social", "l": "TeePublic Paid Social"}, {"go": "rb|channel/Paid Social", "l": "Redbubble Paid Social"}], status: "Watching", tone: "open", channel: "#peak_daily_performance_standup", fx: "Partly: cheaper ads can bring more paid social visitors, which can lower CVR without a site problem.", body: "In the USA, Meta ad costs are down 6 to 8% day over day after the election; Marketing had hoped for 10 to 12% given how high prices rose this year. A lag is possible, so more relief may show in the next few days, and Marketing is monitoring.", tldr: "Meta ads are 6 to 8% cheaper after the election, short of the 10 to 12% hoped for, and may ease further.", pts: ["USA only: 6 to 8% lower media cost day over day.", "Hoped for 10 to 12% because rates rose so much this year.", "Marketing expects some lag and is watching it."], why: "If paid social sessions rise while CVR dips, cheaper ads are the first thing to check." },
  { icon: "🧥", site: "Both", title: "Pushing higher-profit products would add profit dollars but lower the margin percent", when: "Nov 4", url: "https://grid-articore.enterprise.slack.com/archives/C0C9X6MHV89/p1793825995911849", refs: [], status: "Open", tone: "open", channel: "#peak_daily_performance_standup", fx: "No: nothing has changed on the site. It is a question about what the email feed and search results should push.", body: "With cold weather arriving, the group asked whether leaning into fleece would lift gross profit dollars. T-shirts carry the higher margin percent on both brands (TeePublic 52.1% vs 45.7% for fleece over 28 days, Redbubble 55.0% vs 49.0%), while fleece earns more profit dollars per unit ($17.00 vs $10.77). More fleece would lower the overall percent, so it depends which target matters. The email feed would need extra feeds to carry it, and the group agreed it is worth testing, including in search results.", tldr: "Fleece earns more profit dollars per unit but lowers the margin percent; the group wants to test it.", pts: ["T-shirts have the higher margin percent on both brands.", "Fleece earns $17.00 per unit against $10.77 for T-shirts.", "The email feed would need additional feeds to promote it."], qs: ["Which matters more through peak: profit dollars or margin percent?"], why: "If average order value or margin percent shifts in search or email, check whether the product mix changed." },
  { icon: '🔁', site: 'RB', title: 'Redbubble is choosing which product styles search results swap to a cheaper default, with or without a test', when: 'Nov 3', url: 'https://grid-articore.enterprise.slack.com/archives/C0C9X6MHV89', refs: [{ go: 'rb|overall', l: 'Redbubble CVR' }], status: 'Decision needed', tone: 'open', channel: '#peak_daily_performance_standup', fx: 'No: the change is not live yet, so none of the numbers shown include it. It could move Redbubble CVR and revenue per session once it ships.', body: 'Redbubble search result pages already show a default style in place of several variants (matte for the other sticker finishes, the classic tee for seven other tee styles). Engineering can add five more tee styles before code freeze and says other product swaps carry trade-offs. The team asked whether any other high-value swaps are wanted and whether to A/B test, because last-minute certificate security work may leave no time for a test. Marketing and product leads replied that the added tee swaps look good and that rolling out without a test is acceptable if that is the only option.', tldr: 'Redbubble search will swap five more tee styles to the classic tee before code freeze, probably without an A/B test.', pts: ['Already swapped on search pages: three sticker finishes to matte, and seven tee styles to the classic tee.', 'Can be added before code freeze: boxy, oversized, vintage wash, active and relaxed tees.', 'An A/B test may not fit because of the certificate security work, so the change could ship untested.'], why: 'If Redbubble CVR or revenue per session shifts after this ships, check search-page product mix before looking for a site issue.' },
  { icon: '🎯', site: 'TP', title: 'TeePublic adjusted paid search bidding for the sale, then tightened it after a softer Nov 2', when: 'Nov 3', url: 'https://grid-articore.enterprise.slack.com/archives/C0B55VBUHNE/p1793719207236429?thread_ts=1793719143.269589&cid=C0B55VBUHNE', refs: [{ go: 'tp|channel/Paid Search', l: 'TeePublic Paid Search' }, { go: 'tp|overall', l: 'TeePublic CVR' }], status: 'In effect', tone: 'open', channel: '#all-marketing-analytics-ai', fx: 'Partly: looser bidding from Nov 1 brought in more paid search visitors, which can lower CVR without a site problem. The Nov 2 tightening should show up from Nov 3, which is not in the numbers shown yet.', body: 'The marketing analytics daily read for Mon Nov 2 (day 2 of the TeePublic sale) put orders and revenue inside their normal range for a sale day 2, with checkout completion above its range for a second day. CVR and add to cart rate read below the usual Monday range, which the read puts down to more sessions in the denominator. Paid search return targets were lowered at sale launch on Nov 1 to bid harder, then raised again on some campaigns on Nov 2 and bid exploration was pulled from the trending campaigns after CVR dipped.', tldr: 'Paid search bid harder from the sale launch and was pulled back a little on Nov 2 after CVR dipped; orders still landed in their normal range.', pts: ['Nov 1: return targets lowered on the main shopping campaigns to bid harder for the sale.', 'Nov 2: targets raised again on two campaigns and bid exploration removed after a softer CVR day.', 'Sale day 2 orders and revenue were inside their normal range; checkout completion was above it.'], qs: ['How much of the Nov 2 CVR dip was the extra paid search traffic?'], why: 'Read TeePublic Paid Search CVR for Nov 1 and 2 against other sale-launch days, and expect the Nov 2 tightening to show from Nov 3.' },
];

